<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-sets" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Sets, Counting, and Probability</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Students will be able to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Perform basic set operations (union, intersection, and complement).
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use the inclusion-exclusion rule (also called the addition law) to count the number of elements in the union of two sets.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use a Venn diagram to solve counting problems involving three sets.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Identify and use counting techniques such as the addition rule, multiplication rule, combinations, and permutations to count the number of outcomes in an event.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Compute basic probabilities as a ratio of the number of outcomes in an event to the total number of outcomes possible.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Illustrate, compute, and interpret conditional probabilities, including Bayesian problems.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Distinguish between mutually exclusive and independent events.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Solve counting problems using combined methods.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Construct a probability model and compute its expected value.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</objectives>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			Sets, counting, and probability are fundamental concepts in mathematics that are deeply interconnected. Sets provide a structured way to group objects, forming the basis for understanding collections and relationships. Counting techniques, such as permutations and combinations, allow us to determine the number of ways elements can be arranged or selected from sets.
			Probability builds on these principles by quantifying how likely an event is, using the set of all possible outcomes. Expected value then combines probabilities with payoffs to measure what happens on average over many repetitions, such as whether a game is fair. Taken together, these topics form a cohesive framework for analyzing and predicting outcomes in various scenarios, from simple games to complex scientific experiments.
		</p>
	</introduction>

